// Vendor:   RFC 3164 / RFC 5424 syslog (transport)
// Wire:     `<PRI>...` syslog header + message body, single line per
//           record (auto-detects RFC 3164 BSD vs RFC 5424 versioned)
// Upstream: none — `parse_syslog` is a transport-layer parser, run
//           directly off `ingress` (typically the first process in a
//           pipeline whose `input` is `syslog_tcp` (optionally TLS-
//           terminated) or `syslog_udp`)
// Output:   workspace.syslog.{pri, facility, severity, timestamp,
//           hostname, appname, procid, msgid, msg}
//
// Thin wrapper around the `syslog.parse()` primitive. Exists so a
// pipeline can express transport stacking explicitly:
//
//   process parse_syslog | <bridge> | parse_<vocabulary> | compose_<target>
//
// rather than having every vocabulary parser inline `syslog.parse(ingress)`
// against an assumed syslog wire. Bridging the syslog body into a
// vocabulary-specific intake (`workspace.<vocab>.body` etc.) is the
// caller's responsibility — see e.g. parse_openssh's header for
// example bridges.
//
// Sample input (RFC 5424):
//   <38>1 2026-04-30T01:23:45.123456Z host01 sshd 12345 - - Accepted publickey for alice from 192.0.2.10 port 51234 ssh2
//
// Sample input (RFC 3164):
//   <38>Apr 30 01:23:45 host01 sshd[12345]: Accepted publickey for alice from 192.0.2.10 port 51234 ssh2

def process parse_syslog {
    workspace.syslog = syslog.parse(ingress)
}
